# Release & CI

- `ci.yml` runs on macOS/Linux/Windows: `typecheck`, `cargo fmt --check`, clippy,
  `cargo test`, `npm audit`, and `cargo audit`. Keep them green; match the
  toolchain pinned in `.nvmrc`.
- Release artifacts must be built by CI from a tag, signed + notarized (macOS),
  checksummed (`SHA256SUMS`), and shipped with CycloneDX SBOMs and license /
  corresponding-source links. Follow the full checklist in
  `docs/reference/release.md`.
- Before tagging, invoke the project `release-changelog` skill. The release
  commit must synchronize npm/Cargo/Tauri versions, contain a dated
  `CHANGELOG.md` section, and keep `docs/reference/changelog.md` plus
  `docs/reference/release-audit.md` in sync (`make changelog-sync`). CI runs
  `make changelog-check` on every PR and `check-release.mjs` on the tag.
- Release tags must point to commits reachable from `main` or `staging`.
  Staging-origin releases are always prereleases; feature branches never build
  downloadable artifacts. Until the stable gate passes, prefer
  `vX.Y.Z-alpha.N` (semver prerelease). Any hyphenated version is a GitHub
  prerelease and never Latest. Do not retag already-published plain `0.x`
  releases.
- Keep the release matrix serialized while `tauri-action` merges platform
  entries into `latest.json`. Every release must include macOS ARM64/Intel,
  Linux x86_64, Windows x86_64, and the signed privileged helper.
- CI creates drafts only. Publishing, marking Latest, and promoting a Windows
  build from experimental require human artifact/install verification.
- Never put credential values in code, workflows, commits, or logs — only GitHub
  Actions secrets (see `RELEASE_SECRETS.md`). The Tauri updater private key and
  password must be backed up offline; losing them permanently breaks updates.
- Missing macOS/updater secrets must fail the release. Missing Windows signing
  produces an explicitly experimental unsigned artifact that must never be
  labeled stable.
